    en: `
    <p>Norwegian culture has been shaped by dramatic landscapes, the sea, mountains and the country's long Viking heritage. For centuries, communities relied on fishing, farming and seafaring, making <strong>hard work, independence, honesty, cooperation and respect for nature</strong> central values of Norwegian society. Although Norway is now one of the world's wealthiest countries, its people continue to value simplicity, modesty and a close connection with nature rather than displaying wealth.</p>

    <p>A defining feature of Norwegian life is the philosophy of <strong>friluftsliv</strong>, meaning "open-air living." Hiking, skiing, fishing, camping and spending time in the mountains or by the fjords are considered an essential part of everyday life rather than occasional leisure activities. Children are introduced to outdoor life from an early age, and nature remains at the heart of the national identity.</p>

    <p>Norway has made significant contributions to world culture. It is the homeland of playwright <strong>Henrik Ibsen</strong>, composer <strong>Edvard Grieg</strong>, and artist <strong>Edvard Munch</strong>, creator of the famous painting <em>The Scream</em>. Today the country continues to support literature, music, architecture, cinema, design and the visual arts through numerous festivals and cultural institutions.</p>

    <p>The country's most important national celebration is <strong>Constitution Day on 17 May</strong>. Across Norway, colourful parades fill the streets as people proudly wear traditional <strong>bunad</strong> costumes, wave Norwegian flags and celebrate with music and community events. Unlike many national holidays elsewhere, children's parades are the centrepiece of the celebration.</p>

    <p>The majority of Norwegians have traditionally belonged to the <strong>Lutheran Christian</strong> tradition. Although modern Norway is one of Europe's most secular societies, Christianity continues to influence many cultural customs and holidays. Freedom of religion is fully protected, and Islam, Catholicism, Orthodoxy, Buddhism, Hinduism and many other faiths are also represented throughout the country.</p>

    <p>Modern Norwegian society is built upon the principles of <strong>equality, mutual trust, respect for individual rights and environmental responsibility</strong>. High-quality education, a strong welfare system, gender equality and sustainable development have helped make Norway one of the world's best places to live while preserving its rich historical traditions and cultural identity.</p>
  `,
